[section:nsga2 NSGA-II: Multiobjective Optimization]

[heading Synopsis]

``
    #include <boost/math/optimization/nsga2.hpp>

    namespace boost::math::optimization {

    template <typename ArgumentContainer> struct nsga2_parameters {
        using Real = typename ArgumentContainer::value_type;
        using DimensionlessReal = decltype(Real()/Real());
        ArgumentContainer lower_bounds;
        ArgumentContainer upper_bounds;
        size_t population_size = 100;
        size_t max_generations = 250;
        DimensionlessReal crossover_probability = 0.9;
        DimensionlessReal crossover_distribution_index = 15;
        DimensionlessReal mutation_probability = std::numeric_limits<DimensionlessReal>::quiet_NaN();
        DimensionlessReal mutation_distribution_index = 20;
        std::vector<ArgumentContainer> const * initial_population = nullptr;
        unsigned threads = std::thread::hardware_concurrency();
    };

    template <typename ArgumentContainer>
    void validate_nsga2_parameters(nsga2_parameters<ArgumentContainer> const &params);

    template <typename ArgumentContainer, typename ObjectiveContainer, class URBG = std::mt19937_64>
    class nsga2_optimizer {
    public:
        nsga2_optimizer(nsga2_parameters<ArgumentContainer> const &params, URBG gen);
        std::vector<ArgumentContainer> const &ask();
        void tell(std::vector<ObjectiveContainer> values);
        std::vector<std::pair<ArgumentContainer, ObjectiveContainer>> front() const;
        std::vector<ArgumentContainer> const &population() const;
        std::vector<ObjectiveContainer> const &objectives() const;
        std::vector<size_t> const &ranks() const;
    };

    template <typename ArgumentContainer, class Func, class URBG>
    std::vector<std::pair<ArgumentContainer, std::invoke_result_t<Func, ArgumentContainer>>>
    nsga2(const Func objectives,
          nsga2_parameters<ArgumentContainer> const &params,
          URBG &gen,
          std::atomic<bool> *cancellation = nullptr,
          std::vector<std::pair<ArgumentContainer, std::invoke_result_t<Func, ArgumentContainer>>> *queries = nullptr);

    } // namespaces
``

The function `nsga2`, and the class `nsga2_optimizer`, minimize several objectives at once over a box, by the elitist nondominated sorting genetic algorithm of Deb, Pratap, Agarwal and Meyarivan.

A point /x/ ['dominates] a point /y/ if no objective is larger at /x/ than at /y/, and at least one is smaller.
The points that no other point dominates are the Pareto set, and their objectives are the Pareto front: along it, one objective is only improved at the cost of another.
NSGA-II returns a finite set of points, none dominating another, which approximates the Pareto front and is spread along it.

Each generation, parents are chosen by binary tournaments, and their children are made by simulated binary crossover and polynomial mutation, both of which keep to the bounds.
A child which is a copy of a parent, or of another child, is replaced by a new one, unless a generation has already made `population_size` copies.
Parents and children are then sorted into fronts: the points that nothing dominates, the points that only those dominate, and so on.
The next generation is filled front by front; of the last front that does not fit, the members with the largest ['crowding distance], which are those with the most room between their neighbours on the front, are kept.

[heading Parameters]

* `lower_bounds`, `upper_bounds`: The box in which the minimization is done. All bounds must be finite.
* `population_size`: The number of points in each generation, and the most that can be returned. At least 4.
* `max_generations`: The number of generations of children. The objectives are evaluated `population_size * (max_generations + 1)` times.
* `crossover_probability`: The probability that a pair of parents is crossed rather than copied.
* `crossover_distribution_index`: The [eta][sub c] of simulated binary crossover. The larger it is, the closer the children are to their parents.
* `mutation_probability`: The probability that a variable of a child is mutated. The default, NaN, is one over the number of variables.
* `mutation_distribution_index`: The [eta][sub m] of polynomial mutation. The larger it is, the smaller the mutations.
* `initial_population`: An optional list of at most `population_size` points in the bounds, which start the first generation; the rest of it is uniformly distributed in the box.
* `threads`: The number of threads which evaluate the objectives. If the objectives are not threadsafe, set this to 1.

The defaults are those in common use for the algorithm, and are a good place to start.

[heading The function]

Parameters:

* `objectives`: A function of an `ArgumentContainer` which returns a container of the objectives, such as a `std::array<double, 2>` or a `std::vector<double>`. Every objective is minimized; to maximize one, return its negative. The number of objectives is the size of the container, which must be the same, and not zero, at every call.
* `params`: The parameters to the algorithm as described above.
* `gen`: A uniform random bit generator, like `std::mt19937_64`.
* `cancellation`: An optional atomic boolean to allow the user to stop the computation and gracefully return the points found up to then.
    N.B.: Cancellation is not immediate; the in-progress generation finishes.
* `queries`: An optional vector to store each point evaluated, with its objectives, generation by generation, in the order of the population.
    It is written to on the calling thread without a lock, so do not read it from another thread before `nsga2` returns; to follow a run, use `nsga2_optimizer`.

Returns:

The members of the last generation which no other member dominates, as pairs of the argument and its objectives, in lexicographic order of the objectives.
For two objectives, that is from the least value of the first objective to the least value of the second.
Members whose objectives are equal are returned once, so there are between 1 and `population_size` pairs.

A point at which an objective is NaN is dominated by every point at which none is.
Such a point is only returned if an objective is NaN at every member of the last generation.

All random numbers are drawn from `gen` on the calling thread, and only the objectives are evaluated in parallel, so for a given state of `gen` the result does not depend on the number of threads.
As with [link math_toolkit.differential_evolution differential evolution], the threads are worth their overhead once an evaluation of the objectives takes more than about a microsecond.

[heading Ask and tell]

The class `nsga2_optimizer` is the algorithm without the evaluation of the objectives, which is left to the caller: in one batch on a GPU or an FPGA, on a cluster, or however else they are to be had.

* `ask()` returns the points to evaluate: first the initial population, then the children of each generation, `population_size` at a time.
    It returns the same points until their objectives are told, after which they are no longer valid: copy what is needed of them before `tell`.
* `tell(values)` takes the objectives of those points, in their order, and selects the next generation.
    It throws `std::domain_error` if no points were asked for, or if their number or the number of objectives is wrong.
* `front()` is what `nsga2` returns: the nondominated members so far. It is empty before the first `tell`.
* `population()`, `objectives()` and `ranks()` are the whole population after the last `tell`: its `population_size` members, their objectives, and the front each is in, 0 being the nondominated one.

``
    nsga2_optimizer<ArgType, std::array<double, 2>> optimizer(params, std::mt19937_64(12345));
    for (size_t generation = 0; generation <= 250; ++generation) {
        auto const &points = optimizer.ask();
        std::vector<std::array<double, 2>> values = evaluate_all(points);
        optimizer.tell(std::move(values));
        // optimizer.front() can be looked at, saved, or tested for convergence here.
    }
``

The optimizer keeps its own copy of the generator, so it is the whole state of a run.
If `URBG` is a reference type, such as `std::mt19937_64&`, it uses the caller's generator instead, which must outlive it.
The caller's loop decides when to stop, so `max_generations` and `threads` are not used.
The function `nsga2` is this loop with the objectives evaluated on `threads` threads, and for the same state of `gen` gives the same front.

[h4:examples Examples]

Schaffer's problem asks for /x/ to be close to both 0 and 2.
The Pareto set is the interval between them:

``
    using ArgType = std::array<double, 1>;
    auto objectives = [](ArgType const &x) {
        return std::array<double, 2>{x[0] * x[0], (x[0] - 2) * (x[0] - 2)};
    };
    nsga2_parameters<ArgType> params;
    params.lower_bounds = {-10};
    params.upper_bounds = {10};
    std::mt19937_64 gen(12345);
    auto front = nsga2(objectives, params, gen);
    for (auto const &[x, f] : front) {
        std::cout << x[0] << ": " << f[0] << ", " << f[1] << "\n";
    }
``

More examples and API use cases can be studied in [@../../test/test_nsga2.cpp test_nsga2.cpp].

[h5:caveats Caveats]

NSGA-II is appropriate for two or three objectives.
With more, almost every point of a population is nondominated, so the sort no longer separates good points from bad, and the crowding distance is a poor measure of how well a front of three or more dimensions is covered.
Methods which use reference directions, such as NSGA-III, are preferable there.

The arguments are less accurate than the objectives: a point at a distance [delta] from the Pareto set can be worse by only [delta][super 2] in every objective, which a finite population rarely resolves.
A larger population brings the arguments closer.

Constraints other than the bounds are not supported.
Unlike the other optimizers, `nsga2` compares arguments and objectives with `==`, and iterates over the objectives, so both containers need `operator==` and the objectives `begin()` and `end()`.

The algorithm needs many evaluations: tens of thousands is typical.
The sort compares every pair among the parents and children, so each generation costs a multiple of the square of `population_size` beyond the evaluations.

[h4:references References]

* K. Deb, A. Pratap, S. Agarwal and T. Meyarivan, ['A fast and elitist multiobjective genetic algorithm: NSGA-II.] IEEE Transactions on Evolutionary Computation, vol. 6, no. 2, pp. 182-197, April 2002, doi: 10.1109/4235.996017.
* K. Deb and R. B. Agrawal, ['Simulated binary crossover for continuous search space.] Complex Systems, vol. 9, no. 2, pp. 115-148, 1995.
* K. Deb and M. Goyal, ['A combined genetic adaptive search (GeneAS) for engineering design.] Computer Science and Informatics, vol. 26, no. 4, pp. 30-45, 1996.
* K. Deb and H. Jain, ['An evolutionary many-objective optimization algorithm using reference-point-based nondominated sorting approach, part I: solving problems with box constraints.] IEEE Transactions on Evolutionary Computation, vol. 18, no. 4, pp. 577-601, August 2014, doi: 10.1109/TEVC.2013.2281535.

